90,5187,2555,532,59,2533,1986,6696,601,279,2701,37906,1447,16,13,17767,17,64,481,293,284,220,24,59,340,17,13,17767,17,66,481,294,284,220,18,59,340,18,13,17767,64,481,220,18,65,284,220,22,59,340,19,13,17767,66,481,220,18,67,284,481,16,59,692,1654,11625,279,1156,1378,37906,369,17767,64,57758,323,17767,65,59,7731,3830,23606,320,16,1648,17767,65,284,220,17,64,481,220,24,59,340,3830,23606,320,18,1648,17767,65,284,1124,37018,90,64,481,220,22,15170,18,11035,692,24509,1095,279,1378,23393,369,17767,65,59,7731,59,9640,17,64,481,220,24,284,1124,37018,90,64,481,220,22,15170,18,532,59,2533,57251,6711,1526,553,220,18,1447,59,9640,21,64,481,220,17,22,284,264,481,220,22,198,59,2533,49,682,81,8595,3793,1447,59,9640,20,64,284,220,17,15,1124,6383,550,264,284,220,19,198,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,17767,64,284,220,19,57758,1182,1119,17767,65,284,220,17,64,481,220,24,59,7731,59,9640,65,284,220,17,7,19,8,481,220,24,284,220,23,481,220,24,284,481,16,198,59,2533],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix},$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}.$  Express your answer in the form \"$y = mx + b$\".","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6e3d4b58acc23fada5c933b31aaa49ecd8b0425028a970a3798d883ff75bf645"},"seconds":40.96499466896057,"seed":498470671888039813,"stop":"eos","text":"So, \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = -1\\).\n\nNext, we solve the last two equations for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\):\n\nFrom equation (2): \\(d = 2c - 3\\)\nFrom equation (4): \\(d = \\frac{c + 1}{3}\\)\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(d\\):\n\n\\[\n2c - 3 = \\frac{c + 1}{3}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3:\n\n\\[\n6c - 9 = c + 1\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\n5c = 10 \\implies c = 2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(c = 2\\) back into \\(d = 2c - 3\\):\n\n\\[\nd = 2(2) - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(c = 2\\) and \\(d = 1\\).\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). A point \\((x, y)\\) on the line can be written as \\((x, 2x + 1)\\). The image of this point under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet the image point be \\((X, Y)\\). Then:\n\n\\[\nX = 2x - 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Y = 4x + 1\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(X\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\\) into the expression for \\(Y\\):\n\n\\[\nY = 4 \\left( \\frac{X + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(X + 1) + 1 = 2X + 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). Given that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can express \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom the given transformations, we have the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the following equations:\n\n1. \\(2a - b = 9\\)\n2. \\(2c - d = 3\\)\n3. \\(a - 3b = 7\\)\n4. \\(c - 3d = -1\\)\n\nWe solve the first two equations for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\nFrom equation (1): \\(b = 2a - 9\\)\nFrom equation (3): \\(b = \\frac{a - 7}{3}\\)\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n2a - 9 = \\frac{a - 7}{3}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3:\n\n\\[\n6a - 27 = a - 7\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\n5a = 20 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 4\\) back into \\(b = 2a - 9\\):\n\n\\[\nb = 2(4) - 9 = 8 - 9 = -1\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = -1\\).\n\nNext, we solve the last two equations for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\):\n\nFrom equation (2): \\(d = 2c - 3\\)\nFrom equation (4): \\(d = \\frac{c + 1}{3}\\)\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(d\\):\n\n\\[\n2c - 3 = \\frac{c + 1}{3}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3:\n\n\\[\n6c - 9 = c + 1\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\n5c = 10 \\implies c = 2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(c = 2\\) back into \\(d = 2c - 3\\):\n\n\\[\nd = 2(2) - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(c = 2\\) and \\(d = 1\\).\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). A point \\((x, y)\\) on the line can be written as \\((x, 2x + 1)\\). The image of this point under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet the image point be \\((X, Y)\\). Then:\n\n\\[\nX = 2x - 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Y = 4x + 1\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(X\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\\) into the expression for \\(Y\\):\n\n\\[\nY = 4 \\left( \\frac{X + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(X + 1) + 1 = 2X + 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:243"}
